    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2775.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 732,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on Earth.** It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through its chemical makeup, its unique properties, and its role in the universe.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Structure (H₂O)\nScientifically, water is known by the chemical formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms** (H)\n*   **One oxygen atom** (O)\n\nThese atoms are bound together by strong covalent bonds. Because oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen, water is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. This polarity is the secret behind many of water’s amazing properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and find in oceans and rivers.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Unique and Remarkable Properties\nWater behaves very differently from most other liquids on Earth. These anomalies make life possible:\n\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, minerals, and nutrients) than any other liquid. This is how our blood transports nutrients throughout our bodies and how plants absorb food from the soil.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, **expands** and becomes *less* dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float in a glass. In nature, this means lakes freeze from the top down, insulating the water below so aquatic life can survive the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a massive amount of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet (oceans absorb heat from the sun) and helps human bodies maintain a stable internal temperature.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (**cohesion**—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (**adhesion**—which allows water to travel up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Water on Earth and in Life\n*   **Planetary Abundance:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas. However, about 96.5% of that is saltwater. Only a tiny fraction (about 2.5%) is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers and ice caps.\n*   **Biological Necessity:** Life as we know it cannot exist without water. The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It lubricates joints, regulates body temperature, and acts as the medium for all cellular chemical reactions. \n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with complex, miraculous properties that make it the very lifeblood of our planet."
